## 2026-09-22 — Follow-up: 401 on player creation

User reported a 401 when adding a new player and asked whether any request
was accidentally behind authentication.

**Verified not an auth issue:** `AccessGateService` (the passphrase gate)
only controls what renders in the Angular shell (`@if (gate.required &&
!gate.unlocked())` in `app.html`) and never touches `SupabaseService` or
attaches headers to any request. All Supabase calls still go out with just
the public anon key, exactly as before this session's changes.

**Actual cause, found in `src/app/core/services/player.service.ts`:**
`create()` sent `{ display_name, starting_elo }` in the insert payload, but
`supabase/migrations/20260922140800_allow_public_player_creation.sql`
grants `anon` **column-level** INSERT on `display_name` only (deliberately,
so every rating field is forced through the column defaults). Postgres
rejects the extra `starting_elo` column at the privilege level; PostgREST
surfaces that specific permission failure as a 401, which reads like an
auth problem but is really a column-grant mismatch. This predates this
session — it was in the code before the `develop` branch existed — but
wasn't caught when `player.service.ts` was rewritten earlier this session.

**Fix (commit `779baf6`):** stop sending `starting_elo` from the client.
The column already defaults to 520 and has `check (starting_elo = 520)`, so
it can never be anything else anyway — dropping it from the insert avoids
referencing a column `anon` has no grant on, and is also more consistent
with spec section 45 (client never submits authoritative rating fields).

Verified with `tsc --noEmit` (clean). If a 401 persists after pulling this
fix, check: (1) that migration 3
(`20260922140800_allow_public_player_creation.sql`) is actually applied to
the live Supabase project, and (2) that `SUPABASE_URL`/`SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`
in the running build match that project.

