## 2026-09-22 — Follow-up: 20260922160000 failed to apply, constraint-name lookup fixed

Pedro applied `20260922155000_...constraint_race.sql` successfully, then hit
this running `20260922160000_placement_matches_ten.sql`:

```
ERROR: 42710: constraint "players_placement_matches_played_check" for
relation "players" already exists
```

**Root cause:** the migration's dynamic constraint lookup matched on the
literal text `between` inside `pg_get_constraintdef()`'s output. Postgres
deparses `BETWEEN x AND y` back into `(col >= x) AND (col <= y)` when it
reconstructs a constraint's definition -- the word "between" never
survives that round-trip. So the lookup's first pattern never matched the
original `placement_matches_played between 0 and 5` constraint, leaving it
in place; the migration's own `add constraint
players_placement_matches_played_check` then collided with it, because
Postgres auto-names an unnamed column-level CHECK as
`<table>_<column>_check` -- exactly the name this migration also chose.
(The lookup's *second* pattern, matching `placement_complete = (...)`, did
fire and dropped that constraint successfully -- so the failure was
partial, and the two ADD CONSTRAINT clauses being one atomic ALTER TABLE
statement meant neither new constraint got created.)

**Fix, in place in `20260922160000_placement_matches_ten.sql`:** the lookup
now matches any check constraint on `players` whose definition mentions the
column name `placement_matches_played` at all, regardless of how Postgres
chose to spell the comparison. This one pattern catches both of the
original constraints, and is safe to re-run (it will find, and idempotently
replace, the constraints this same migration adds) and safe to run again
against a database already left in the partial state above by the earlier,
broken attempt (it simply finds one constraint to drop instead of two).

No other file changed. `record_match` was never reached by the earlier
failed run (it's a separate, later statement in the same file, and the
ALTER TABLE error stopped execution before it), so the database is still
correctly on the `20260922155000` fix at a threshold of 5 -- re-running the
corrected `20260922160000` is the only remaining step.

